# Seven defences, seven mutations, each red on its own named test. 2026-09-10.
#
# The restore baseline is the COMMITTED fix, the commit that carries this file,
# so the runner restores with `git checkout -- <path>`, which would have thrown
# the fix away had this been run before the commit. No hash is quoted: this file
# was written between the commit and its amend, and a hash written here would
# name the orphan.
#
# Runner: scripts kept in the session scratchpad. It counts the pattern before
# touching anything and stops unless it is hit exactly once, then writes the
# mutation and asserts the replacement is present before running vitest, so a
# mutation that fails to land reports an error instead of a green suite. The
# second assert is inside the try whose finally restores the file; the first is
# above it, which is where it belongs, since nothing has been written yet.
#
# It also refuses to touch a file that `git status --porcelain` reports as
# dirty. That guard exists because this runner's restore step is
# `git checkout -- <path>`, a whole-file overwrite from HEAD, and running it on
# a file carrying uncommitted work destroys that work silently and exits 0. It
# happened to avatarPitch.test.ts on this change, between the second review
# round and its commit.
#
# One scope for all seven:
#   npx vitest run src/components/chat/avatarPitch.test.ts
#
# The file held 31 tests when this was captured. It grew the same day, when the
# mode-driven gaze went into it (aim-0910.md), and all seven were re-run there
# against the larger file with the same result; that output is in
# aim-0910-mutations.log.
#
#   P1  nod        o.hp += fwd * Math.sin(p * Math.PI * 2) * 0.14 * env
#                        -> o.hp += Math.sin(p * Math.PI * 2) * 0.14 * env
#   P2  bounce     o.sx += fwd * w * 0.05   ->  o.sx += w * 0.05
#   P3  bounce     o.hp += fwd * w * 0.05   ->  o.hp += w * 0.05
#   P4  toeLook    o.hp += fwd * env * 0.3  ->  o.hp += env * 0.3
#   P5  tilt       apply: (_p, env, v, o) => {  o.hr += v * 0.16 * env
#                        ->  apply: (_p, env, v, o, fwd) => {
#                                   o.hr += fwd * v * 0.16 * env
#   P6  the test   BLIND_SAMPLES = [0, 0.125, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]
#                        ->  [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1]
#   P7  the test   drop 'hipTwist' from VERSION_BLIND, so one gesture belongs
#                  to neither list
#
# P2 and P3 are separate because `bounce` writes the same weight twice, on the
# spine and on the head. Dropping either factor alone leaves the other's test
# green, which is why one assertion for the gesture would have pinned only half
# of it.
#
# P5 CHANGES THE SIGNATURE AS WELL AS THE TERM, on purpose. A first version
# replaced only `v * 0.16 * env`, and `tilt`'s apply does not declare `fwd`, so
# the mutant threw `ReferenceError: fwd is not defined` and reddened two tests
# by crashing. A crash proves nothing about an assertion. The mutation above is
# the whole, compilable over-application someone signing "every axis" would
# write, and it reddens the one test that is about the facing.
#
# P6 and P7 mutate the TEST rather than the engine, because what they defend is
# the test's own coverage. P6: `wiggle` is sin(4*pi*p), so on the quarter points
# alone every sample lands on one of its own zeros and the version-blind
# comparison would have been between two pieces of floating-point residue. It
# was written that way, and a reviewer found it. P7: the two lists have to
# account for every name in the table, or an eleventh gesture arrives covered by
# nothing while every test here stays green. A reviewer found that one too.
#
# P7's tally reads 29 rather than 31 because dropping a gesture from
# VERSION_BLIND also removes the two tests that gesture generates. The point is
# the single FAIL, not the total.
#
# RESULT. One red per mutation, seven distinct tests, no extras.
#
